      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-h/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(i) The leakage law is dV/dt = −k√x, and dV/dt = (dV/dx)(dx/dt) with V = 5x3/3. (a) Using dV/dx = 5x2, [M1] obtain 5x2 dx/dt = −k√x. [A1] Dividing by √x [A1] gives 5x3/2 dx/dt = −k . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-h/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(ii) Separate 5x3/2 dx = −k dt. R 5x3/2 dx = R −k dt to get 2x5/2 = −kt + C. [M1] [A1] The full tank at t = 0, x = 4, Integrate [A1] gives 2(4)5/2 = −k(0) + C, hence C = 64. [A1] At t = 32, x = 0, [A1] so 0 = 64 −32k, hence k = 2. [A1] Then 2x5/2 = 64 −2t, 2t = 64 −2x5/2, giving t = 32 −x5/2 . [A1] 2 −2√x. [B1] [A1] Equating it to 5x2 dx/dt gives 5x2(dx/dt) = 1/2 −2√x, hence (i) Net flow is 1 (b) 10x2(dx/dt) = 1 −4√x and dx/dt = (1 −4√x)/(10x2) . [A1] (ii) An equilibrium satisfies dx/dt = 0, requiring 1 −4√x = 0. Thus 4√x = 1, √x = 1/4, x = 1/16. The height rises when 0 &lt; x &lt; 1/16, and x = 1/16 is an equilibrium: [B1] The right-hand side is smooth near that equilibrium, so a trajectory starting below it cannot cross it: Thus the upper bound is 1/16 m . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
